   249	`build_descriptor_driver_unsupported`, and against an unsupported descriptor
   250	version with `build_descriptor_schema_unsupported`. Neither failure may fall
   251	back to another target, another driver, `go-v1`, a script, a system command, or
   252	a generic build facility.
   253	
   254	The command and descriptor drivers MUST be equal, and both MUST name the same
   255	external identifier. The whole repository snapshot remains the validation,
   256	identity, and audit subject; only the selected build root is compiler-visible;
   257	and no external repository byte is agent-facing or runtime-copied. The
   258	schema-6 prohibition on a local `build_root` equal to `.` is unaffected by the
   259	descriptor's admission of `.` as a repository root.
   260	
   261	### 6. Toolchain boundary
   262	
   263	The manifest toolchain requirement, trusted resolution, version grammar and
   264	comparison, two-stage preflight ordering, diagnostics, and installation-guidance
   265	catalog are defined by the shared toolchain contract, decision 0007
   266	(`TASK-260728-1g0z69`), and integrated by `TASK-260728-2jaw7h`. This decision
   267	does not restate or redefine them. It fixes only the boundary properties the
   268	version and artifact model depends on:
   269	
   270	1. Every new driver's canonical build input MUST bind a complete trusted
   271	   toolchain identity produced by that contract. `curator-go-toolchain-v1` stays
   272	   frozen and Go-only; no other driver may reuse, extend, or alias it.
   273	2. The trusted toolchain is operator-owned. No manifest, descriptor, repository,
   274	   substitution, or environment value may supply a toolchain path, URL, channel,
   275	   version pin, mirror, trust root, installer, or package-manager command, and no
   276	   version of any driver may auto-install a toolchain.
   277	3. Every executable started below the worker MUST be a fingerprinted member of
   278	   the driver's declared trusted toolchain closure. When a compiler requires a
   279	   platform linker, SDK, sysroot, runtime library, or archiver that is not inside
   280	   the fingerprinted distribution, the driver MUST bind that component into its
   281	   toolchain identity or MUST reject that platform. A host-resolved tool outside
   282	   the closure is not admissible.
   283	4. Because the toolchain identity is inside the canonical build input, two
   284	   toolchains never alias in the cache, the receipt, the marker, or a claim.
   285	5. Host availability and version preflight runs before source acquisition, and
   286	   the project-metadata compatibility cross-check runs after local validation or
   287	   exact external acquisition and audit and before any compiler work. This
   288	   ordering is owned by the toolchain contract and is restated here only because
   289	   the artifact and audit boundary assumes it.
   290	
   291	### 7. Execution policy and process graph
   292	
   293	All eight drivers run under the existing portable `manager-worker-v1` execution
   294	policy. No new execution-policy identity is minted, the mandatory portable
   295	control set of `protocol/core.md` section 4.2.1 is unchanged, the exhaustive
   296	`rc5-native-control-inventory-v1` inventory is unchanged because no control is
   297	added, removed, or re-scoped, and the closed `capability-evidence-v1` record is
   298	unchanged.
   299	
   300	The fixed process graph is restated once, generically, without changing what it
   301	requires:
   302	
   303	```text
   304	manager parent
   305	  -> identity-verified manager-owned worker
   305	  -> identity-verified manager-owned worker
   306	       -> the driver's fingerprinted trusted launcher
   307	            -> fingerprinted regular executables inside that driver's
   308	               fingerprinted trusted toolchain closure
   309	```
   310	
   311	The two lower nodes were always per-operation values bound by the toolchain
   312	identity inside the build input rather than constants of the policy; for `go-v1`
   313	and `go-repository-v1` they resolve exactly as before to `<GOROOT>/bin/go` and
   314	`<GOROOT>/pkg/tool/<GOHOSTOS>_<GOHOSTARCH>/`. Every `go-v1` and
   315	`go-repository-v1` canonical build input, logical cache key, receipt byte
   316	sequence, marker record, and claim is therefore unchanged by this decision.
   317	
   318	One worker session performs exactly one read-only graph phase of at most one
   319	driver-defined command, waits while the parent validates the complete graph,
   320	accepts exactly one authenticated build permit, and performs exactly one
   321	compile phase of exactly one driver-defined command. The session admits no
   322	retry, second graph phase, second compile phase, additional executable, shell,
   323	VCS operation, dependency download, generator, test, run, or tool request. A
   324	driver that cannot map its pipeline onto that session shape MUST NOT be admitted
   325	in this version; widening the session shape requires a new execution-policy
   326	identity, a new claim schema version, and its own review.
   327	
   328	Before the compile phase, each driver MUST apply an exhaustive, deterministic,
   329	pre-compile rejection matrix computed from the validated snapshot and its graph
   330	phase, in the same position where `go-v1` rejects `SysoFiles`, native inputs,
   331	and the non-standard `//go:cgo_import_dynamic` directive. The matrix MUST reject
   332	every package-selected code-execution surface for that language, including build
   333	scripts, procedural and compiler macros, compiler and build-system plugins,
   334	annotation processors, source generators, manifest programs, build tasks and
   335	recipes, response files, package-selected linkers and native libraries, and
   336	network or registry access. A surface that cannot be rejected deterministically
   337	before the compile phase MUST cause the driver to be rejected, and MUST NOT be
   338	answered by a runtime allowance, an advisory warning, or a sandbox promise. The
   339	shared semantic class for such a rejection is
   340	`build_package_code_execution_forbidden`; each driver contract defines its own
   341	per-surface diagnostics beneath it.
   342	
   343	### 8. Cache, receipt, marker, and claim identity
   344	
   345	`curator-build-source-v1` is unchanged and is reused as the source identity for
   346	every driver in both source modes. It hashes bytes, not language, so no new
   347	source-identity algorithm is created. The protected external snapshot key of
   348	`protocol/core.md` section 9.4 is likewise unchanged.
   349	
   350	Each logical cache key remains the SHA-256 of `CCJ-1` over the complete build
   351	input. Every new build input MUST bind its receipt schema version, driver,
   352	source state for its mode, consuming command name, build root and source
   353	directory selection, native target, complete trusted toolchain identity, and a
   354	closed per-driver policy object that includes the execution-policy identity.
   355	Inputs from different drivers cannot alias, because `driver` and the policy
   356	object differ.
   357	
   358	Build receipt schema 3 covers the local source mode and schema 4 covers the
   359	external source mode, each as a strict `oneOf` discriminated by the `driver`
   360	`const`, and each carrying that driver's own toolchain identity, native target,
   361	and closed policy object. Receipt schemas 1 and 2 keep their bytes and meanings.
   362	
   363	Install marker schema 4 permits `skill_schema_version` through 8 and represents
   364	local-only, external-only, and mixed command sets across receipt schemas 1
   365	through 4. Every build entry MUST explicitly record its driver, its
   366	`receipt_schema_version`, and its `execution_policy`, and a reader MUST validate
   367	the recorded receipt version against the closed driver table of section 2 and
   368	reject a mismatch rather than infer an absent value from a driver name. Marker
   369	schemas 1 through 3 keep their shapes.
   370	
   371	Marker v4 generalizes exactly one schema-6 rule. Top-level `build_source` is
   372	REQUIRED exactly when at least one active local build command of any admitted
   373	local driver exists, and MUST otherwise be absent; marker v3's `go-v1`-only
   374	wording does not survive into v4, and marker v3 itself is unchanged. External
   375	entries continue to bind source per entry and MUST NOT use the consuming skill's
   376	raw snapshot as external compiled-source identity.
   377	
   378	Conformance claim schema 4 pins `protocol_version` to the rc.6 candidate and
   379	admits driver assertions for the eight identifiers, each requiring `driver`,
   380	`language`, `execution_policy`, and `operating_systems`. It admits only
   381	`manager-worker-v1`, so a hardened claim remains structurally impossible and
   382	needs a later claim version. Claim schemas 1 through 3 keep their bytes.
   383	
   384	### 9. Mixed commands, platform claims, credentials, and signing
   385	
   386	Manifest schema 8 MAY mix script commands, system commands, and build commands
   387	of any admitted driver in one manifest and one closure node. Activation,
   388	dependency-command selection, portable-name collision, shim collision, and
   389	provider-first closure rules are unchanged, and active build command names are
   390	still processed in Unicode-scalar lexical order within a closure node. Each
   470	
   471	The stable failure classes of decision 0005 and the `build_execution_*`
   472	diagnostics of decision 0006 continue to apply unchanged to every driver.
   473	
   474	## Rejected alternatives
   475	
   476	- **One driver identifier per language covering both source modes.** Rejected:
   477	  the local and external modes have different source identities, receipts, audit
   478	  subjects, and lifecycle rules, exactly as `go-v1` and `go-repository-v1` do,
   479	  and collapsing them would make the receipt version unreadable from the wire.
   480	- **A generic `native-v1` driver parameterized by a language field, or language
   481	  detection from project-metadata filenames.** Rejected: it is the generic
   482	  fallback that decisions 0004 and 0005 and `protocol/core.md` section 12.3
   483	  forbid, and detection would let repository layout choose a compiler.
   484	- **One receipt schema version per driver, giving six new receipt schemas.**
   485	  Rejected: it multiplies frozen artifacts without adding discrimination, since
   486	  the driver `const` already discriminates inside one schema and the marker
   487	  records the receipt version explicitly.
   488	- **Reusing receipt schemas 1 and 2 for the new drivers.** Rejected: it would
   489	  change frozen rc.4 and rc.5 schema bytes and let a reader that only knows Go
   490	  believe it understands a Rust, Swift, or Kotlin receipt.
   491	- **Admitting a `runtime-bundle` artifact class so Kotlin/JVM could ship.**
   492	  Rejected for the four reasons in section 3. The honest consequence is that
   493	  Kotlin is admitted only through a native backend or not at all, and that
   494	  outcome is `TASK-260728-168smo`'s to establish, not to negotiate away.
   495	- **Letting a driver publish a sidecar runtime or redistributable library
   496	  alongside the executable on platforms that need one.** Rejected: it is the
   497	  runtime-bundle class under a different name, and it would make the artifact
   498	  digest describe only part of what runs.
   499	- **Adding package-controlled members such as a binary, product, feature,
   500	  profile, or configuration to the local command or the descriptor target.**
   501	  Rejected: the consuming command key is the sole executable name, and any
   502	  additional selector hands output and pipeline control back to untrusted data.
   503	- **Minting `manager-worker-v2` for the additional drivers.** Rejected: the
   504	  mandatory control set, worker session discipline, native-control inventory,
   505	  and evidence record are identical, so a new identity would change every `go-v1`
   506	  cache key and invalidate the frozen rc.5 candidate for no security gain.
   507	- **Versioning the native-control inventory or the capability-evidence record
   508	  alongside the new drivers.** Rejected: inventory membership is unchanged, and
   509	  it never enters a build input, artifact, or hashed identity, so a rename would
   510	  carry no semantic content.
   511	- **Bumping `Skillfile.dev.json` to schema 3.** Rejected: substitution selects
   512	  source acquisition only and cannot express a driver, toolchain, target, or
   513	  artifact class.
   514	- **Deferring the version and artifact boundary until the three driver contracts
   515	  are written.** Rejected: each contract would then choose its own versions,
   516	  artifact shape, and descriptor evolution, and the first one merged would define
   517	  the wire by accident.
   518	- **Allowing each driver to state its own execution-policy identity.** Rejected:
   519	  policy identity is a security-strength statement, and per-driver identities
   520	  would let one driver's weaker contract be read as the portable policy.
   521	
