    37	## Decision
    38	
    39	`go-v1` and `go-repository-v1` normatively identify one named execution policy.
    40	Protocol 1.0 defines exactly one value, `manager-worker-v1`, and every
    41	conforming manager implements it on macOS and Windows.
    42	
    43	The fixed process graph gains one node:
    44	
    45	```text
    46	manager parent
    47	  -> identity-verified manager-owned worker
    48	       -> fingerprinted <GOROOT>/bin/go
    49	            -> fingerprinted regular executables below
    50	               <GOROOT>/pkg/tool/<GOHOSTOS>_<GOHOSTARCH>/
    51	```
    52	
    53	The worker is an exact re-execution of the installed manager executable in one
    54	fixed hidden mode. It is a security boundary, not a command surface: no package
    55	file, manifest, descriptor, environment value, `PATH` entry, shell, or user
    56	option selects it. One session runs exactly one `go list`, waits for the parent
    57	to validate the complete package graph, accepts exactly one authenticated build
    58	permit, and runs exactly one `go build`.
    59	
    60	The mandatory portable controls, the exhaustive native-control inventory, the six
    61	deferred hardened guarantees, the closed capability-evidence record, and the
    62	package-influence exclusions are normative in `protocol/core.md` section 4.2.1,
   125	  without adding any portable guarantee, and it invites a reader to treat a key
   126	  as capability proof.
   127	- **Add the execution policy to marker v2.** Rejected: marker v2 keeps its
   128	  frozen shape, and the cache key and receipt hash it already records bind the
   129	  execution policy transitively. Marker v3, which is new in this release, records
   130	  it explicitly.
   131	- **Reuse `go-v1` semantics without a policy identity.** Rejected: a semantic
   132	  change to the process graph without a cache-identity change would let a
   133	  pre-revision entry alias a portable entry.
   134	- **Keep "every native control the host provides" as the rule.** Rejected: an
   135	  open-ended rule lets a new operating-system primitive, or a different reading
   136	  of "provides", change conformance without changing the policy identity, and it
   137	  lets two conforming managers emit incomparable evidence. The inventory is
   138	  therefore exhaustive, versioned, and normative per platform, and the evidence
   139	  record is closed. Extending the inventory is a specification revision with a
   140	  new inventory version; it is not an execution-policy revision, because

